// frob.strata -- frob's own architecture, modeled in its own language
// (phase 4 self-hosting exit criterion, T-0081, docs/strata/roadmap.md
// "Self-hosting commitments (decision D7)"). This supersedes the informal
// module-dependency diagram in docs/rework.md as enforced truth: `frob
// check --only sys` gates on the directives below resolving into THIS
// model, on (T-0080) cross-component imports respecting the flows
// declared here wherever tier-2 `code=` binding is in force, and (T-0150)
// on each node's `code`/`may` declarations below matching what `frob sys
// audit`'s SYS100/SYS101/SYS102 self-conformance checks actually observe
// in `src/frob/`.
//
// Every node below declares `code "<glob>" ...;` (tier-2 code binding,
// T-0078/T-0132) and `may "<capability>";` (declared capability surface,
// T-0079/T-0132) directly -- both ARE reachable from `.strata` source text
// today (T-0132 landed the STRING-quoted `code`/`may` grammar in
// `strata-core/src/parse.rs::parse_node`, `docs/strata/surface.md#node-
// grammar-implemented-t-0132-closes-the-code-may-gap-t-0136-adds-on-
// deploy`). A PRIOR version of this file's header claimed otherwise --
// that claim was already stale when written (T-0132 landed before this
// file's merge-base) and has been corrected here; see T-0150's Done
// report for the full correction record. `may` values were measured
// honestly via `frob.vet._capability.scan_file_capabilities` against every
// file each node's `code` globs bind (T-0150); a "may" atom's kind is the
// segment before its first `.`/`:` (`_effects.py::_may_kind`), so a bare
// kind name like `"net"`/`"fs"`/`"exec"` (the same three names THREAT004's
// `_KIND_MAP` already normalizes net/fs-write/exec sinks to) or
// `"eval"`/`"env"`/`"ffi"`/`"install-hook"` (vet's own kind names, which
// have no tier-2 line-level analog -- T-0150's SYS100 extended case) is
// declared as-is.
//
// Every flow below is a real import edge this repo's `src/frob/*`
// packages actually have today (computed by walking `from frob.X import`/
// `import frob.X` across every file and rolling the 25+ leaf packages up
// into the 8 components docs/strata/roadmap.md names for phase 4:
// cli/app layer, graph+lang, gates, tickets, check, strata, dup+frob-core,
// vet). Where two components import each other in both directions, both
// flows are declared -- this model does not pretend the real dependency
// graph is a DAG when it is not.
//
// PII posture (T-0154, std.pii): EXPLICITLY ZERO. frob processes design
// text, ticket text, and code -- not personal data. No node/store below
// declares `carries "<category>.<field>"`, and that absence is checked,
// not assumed: `tests/unit/strata/test_pii.py::
// TestFrobSelfModelPiiPosture` asserts zero `pii=` attrs on every node
// here AND that `_pii.py::evaluate_pii` reports zero PII001-004
// violations, so a future edit that silently starts modeling personal
// data (e.g. a `registry` node gaining an author-email store) fails that
// test loudly rather than the zero case just happening to hold by
// omission. The one genuinely personal datum anywhere near this repo --
// git commit author name/email -- is process/VCS metadata `git` itself
// already records outside `design/`'s scope entirely, the same reasoning
// every other node's `attrs` already applies to "things this model does
// not represent" (docs/strata/threat.md#compliance zero-case precedent:
// an explicit exclusion, never a silent one).

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
module frob

// -- components --------------------------------------------------------

// The only foreign-trust node: third-party package registries (PyPI/npm/
// crates.io/OSV) that `frob.vet` queries over the network
// (src/frob/vet/_registry.py, _osv.py, _popular_*.py). Nothing else in
// this model talks to the network.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node registry : foreign { clearance Public; }

// cli/app layer: src/frob/__main__.py, src/frob/app/**. The single
// entrypoint every subcommand dispatches through (app.py's
// `_dispatch_table`); every other component is reached only via a runner
// in this layer. `may`: T-0150 measured `eval` (bare `compile(` matches --
// see the filed T-0151-class scanner-precision ticket in this ticket's
// Done report, not fixed here) and `fs` (config/log file writes) across
// every file `code` binds.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node cli : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    // T-0500 (docs/audits/strata.md G4): src/frob/__init__.py (package
    // glue re-exporting gitio/excludes, T-0362), src/frob/doctor.py (the
    // `frob doctor` diagnostic subcommand), and src/frob/excludes.py
    // (scan-exclusion helpers used repo-wide) are loose top-level files
    // with no subdirectory of their own -- tightening SYS102 to fire per
    // FOREIGN file (not just per fully-foreign top-level directory)
    // surfaced that none of the three had ever been given a code= glob
    // owner at all. `cli` already owns __main__.py, the other single-file
    // top-level entrypoint, so it is the existing-convention home for
    // this class of file rather than inventing a new node for three
    // small files.
    code "src/frob/app/**" "src/frob/__main__.py" "src/frob/__init__.py"
        "src/frob/doctor.py" "src/frob/excludes.py";
    may "eval";
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    // T-0178: frob.app.telemetry reads FROB_NO_TELEMETRY/FROB_STATS_AGENTIC
    // (os.environ.get) to opt out of/into the non-gated agentic telemetry
    // stream and its `--agentic` report trigger.
    may "env";
    // T-0474: `frob ticket start` detaches its pre-work sweep as a
    // background `frob ticket sweep` child (subprocess.Popen of
    // sys.executable in app/ticket_runner.py::_spawn_background_sweep).
    // Same T-0200 discipline as the checker node: the spawn honors the
    // real, live FROB_DISABLE_EXEC kill switch (frob.process._guard.
    // exec_enabled) by degrading to the synchronous in-process sweep, so
    // this is a switchable capability, not an aspirational claim.
    may "exec";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
    // T-0724: SYS203 (shared store write) fires because cli/core/fleet/
    // gates all have a Flow into tickets_ledger -- correctly, per the
    // grammar's mode-blind "any inbound flow counts as a write" framing
    // (docs/strata/host.md#resource-contention-sys2xx-t-0699). The ledger
    // genuinely HAS an arbiter: every writer serializes through the
    // `.frob/tickets.lock` flock (T-0458 single-writer CLI, hardened by
    // T-0633's locked wholesale ops) -- SYS203 just cannot express that
    // arbitration until T-0700's access-modes+arbitrated_by grammar
    // lands. Re-evaluate at T-0700.
    waive "SYS203:tickets_ledger" reason "all ledger writers serialize through .frob/tickets.lock (T-0458/T-0633); mode-blind until T-0700 arbitrated_by can express this -- re-evaluate at T-0700" ticket "T-0700";
}

// graph+lang: src/frob/graph/**, src/frob/lang/**, src/frob/arch/**. Owns
// parsing (tree-sitter walkers + the strata-core native module), the
// symbol graph snapshot, and per-language digest extraction that every
// other component reads.
// T-0158: `may "sql"` added -- graph/cache.py's sqlite cache writer matches
// the registry's python/sql DangerousOperation (string-formatted
// cursor.execute), a real (if benign, parameterization-owned) observed
// capability the new exhaustive registry now surfaces that the coarser
// old pattern table never patterned.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node graphlang : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/graph/**" "src/frob/lang/**" "src/frob/arch/**";
    may "eval";
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    may "sql";
    // T-0640: graph_cache's fill/invalidation flows are `_infra.py::
    // _cache_node_and_fill_flow`/`_invalidation_flow`-synthesized edges
    // with no surface `attr` clause of their own to mark `local` (an
    // in-process in-memory cache, not a real cross-boundary call) --
    // a CacheDecl attr-forwarding surface is a follow-up ticket, not
    // this one's scope.
    waive "REL200:graph_cache__fill" reason "elaborator-synthesized cache fill flow, in-process in-memory cache with no attr-forwarding surface yet -- T-0845 is the follow-on ticket to add one" ticket "T-0845";
    waive "REL200:graph_cache__inval_f_parse" reason "elaborator-synthesized cache invalidation flow, in-process in-memory cache with no attr-forwarding surface yet -- T-0845 is the follow-on ticket to add one" ticket "T-0845";
}

// gates: src/frob/gates/**. The obligation-graph rule engine (SYS/DRIFT/
// COV/task-tracking/etc.); reads graph+lang's snapshot and strata's design models,
// writes nothing on its own (violations are reported, not auto-filed --
// T-0086's `frob sys plan` is the future ticket-filing seam).
// T-0151: `may "eval"` REMOVED here. It was measured from vet's capability
// scanner matching bare `compile(` against this package's `re.compile(`
// calls (_FRONTMATTER_RE, _AD_ID_RE, _TODO_RE, etc -- all regex, zero real
// eval/exec/dynamic-import anywhere in src/frob/gates/**, confirmed by
// direct grep). The scanner's `compile(` needle now only counts a BARE
// builtin call (T-0151, src/frob/vet/_capability.py), so this package no
// longer observes "eval" and the declaration would otherwise be a stale,
// unfalsifiable claim -- exactly what SYS101 exists to catch.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node gates : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/gates/**";
    // T-0207: `_pii_structural.py` (the SEC110 detector) references
    // `os.environ`/`os.getenv` as the scan-pattern data it matches AGAINST
    // -- the capability scanner observes an `env` capability there. Declared
    // honestly-to-the-measurement (a sound may-analysis over-approximation:
    // the scanner cannot prove gates never reads env), matching how every
    // other node's `may` set is measured from `scan_directory_capabilities`.
    may "env";
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    // T-0443: the DOC004 console/bash command-drift tier resolves the
    // `[[docblocks.commands]]` parser source by dotted path via
    // importlib.import_module (gates/_docblocks.py) -- a dynamic-import
    // (eval-family) capability the scanner rightly observes. The import
    // target comes from tracked frob.toml, not user input, and failures
    // fail open to the unbound warning path.
    may "eval";
    // T-0724: same SYS203/tickets_ledger disposition as `cli` above --
    // the `.frob/tickets.lock` flock (T-0458/T-0633) is the real,
    // untracked-by-the-grammar arbiter; re-evaluate at T-0700.
    waive "SYS203:tickets_ledger" reason "all ledger writers serialize through .frob/tickets.lock (T-0458/T-0633); mode-blind until T-0700 arbitrated_by can express this -- re-evaluate at T-0700" ticket "T-0700";
}

// check orchestration: src/frob/check/**. Runs the gate suite plus the
// process-spawned tool integrations (ruff/mypy/pytest via `frob.process`)
// and folds results into one exit code.
// T-0200 (closing T-0155's honest LINT004 gap): `may "exec"` now has a
// REAL, checked-in kill switch -- `frob.process._guard.
// guarded_subprocess_run` (`src/frob/process/_guard.py`), an env-var-gated
// wrapper every `_run_*` tool runner in `src/frob/check/_python.py`/
// `_native.py`/`_ts.py` calls instead of `subprocess.run` directly.
// Setting `FROB_DISABLE_EXEC=1` genuinely stops every process this
// component spawns (`tests/unit/test_process_guard.py`), live and without
// a redeploy -- this is no longer an aspirational claim (T-0150/T-0151
// discipline: `attr flag=<id>` names an identifier that is real, not a
// mechanism that does not exist), so the T-0174 `waive "LINT004"` is
// retired in favor of the real declaration below.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node checker : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/check/**";
    may "exec";
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
}

// strata itself: src/frob/strata/**, strata-core/**. Parses/elaborates/
// evaluates `.strata` design models (this file included) and does tier-2
// code-binding/effect extraction, which is why it depends on `vet`'s
// capability pattern tables (`_effects.py` reuses `frob.vet._capability`).
// `code` covers only the python half (`strata-core/**`'s rust source is
// outside `bind_code`'s `*.py` walk, T-0078 scope).
// T-0769: `may "net"` (formerly here, T-0155/T-0174's LINT004 waiver
// pending T-0200's kill switch) is DROPPED -- a strictness-increasing
// narrowing, not a relaxation. T-0769's docstring/comment-prose exclusion
// fix proved this module's `net` capability was NEVER genuinely observed
// on real code; the only thing that ever satisfied SYS101 here was a
// docstring false positive in `_effects.py`'s own module docstring
// (`requests.post(...)`-shaped prose describing a hypothetical example),
// now reworded. A direct scan of every file under `src/frob/strata/**`
// confirms zero real net-capable code today. If this package genuinely
// grows a net-capable call in the future, `may "net"` must be
// re-declared then, together with a real kill-switch mechanism (T-0200)
// -- not carried forward speculatively on the strength of a false
// positive. See T-draft-22aa6efc for the discovery/investigation this
// narrowing resolves.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node stratamod : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/strata/**";
    may "eval";
    may "ffi";
    // T-0017 (graphite adoption): `load_repo_benign_capabilities`
    // (`_threat.py`) reads a consuming repo's `frob.toml` -- a real,
    // observed `fs`/`fs-read` site (`Path.open("rb")`/`tomllib.load`),
    // not previously exercised anywhere else in this package.
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
}

// T-0707: the unified registry-entry model (T-0407, `frob.registry`) --
// one typed shape every `docs/design/registry/*.yaml` manifest instantiates
// (compliance/patterns/weaknesses/secrets/pii corpora), so a registry
// entry's schema and disposition grammar live in exactly one place instead
// of being re-derived ad hoc per consuming gate. Distinct from the
// `registry` node above: THAT node is the foreign-trust boundary for
// third-party PACKAGE registries (PyPI/npm/crates, `src/frob/vet/
// _registry.py`); THIS node is an internal, trusted model/loader package
// with no network surface at all -- naming collision in the domain, not
// in this model, so `registry_model` avoids conflating the two.
// `may` measured the same honest way every other node's header here
// documents (direct grep of `src/frob/registry/**`, T-0707 Done report):
// `fs`/`fs-read` (`_models.py`/`_corpus.py`/`_staleness.py` all
// `Path.read_text` the tracked `docs/design/registry/*.yaml` manifests;
// `_corpus.py::append_entry` and `_staleness.py`'s rewrite path both
// `Path.write_text` back to the SAME tracked manifest files -- never an
// arbitrary path, always the registry file the caller already loaded).
// `fs-write` is declared as plain `fs` here, not the separate `fs-write`
// kind, matching every other node/store in this model (`_effects.py`'s
// `_KIND_MAP` normalizes the write-shaped sink to `fs`, so no node here
// declares `fs-write` on its own -- doing so trips SYS101 as a capability
// this scanner never actually reports). No `eval`/`exec`/`net`/`sql`/
// `fetch_url`/`ffi` call exists anywhere under this package (verified by
// direct grep) -- it never talks to a network registry itself, it only
// parses/writes the repo's own tracked YAML.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node registry_model : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/registry/**";
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
}

// T-0707 (fleet half, filed separately at T-0708 then resolved directly
// here since this ticket's mandated gate-green check cannot pass while
// EITHER unmodeled directory remains): the multi-repo fleet orchestrator
// (T-0573, `frob.fleet`) -- `load_manifest`/`collect_status`/`rollup`/
// `route_ticket` over `fleet.toml`, the `frob fleet` subcommand's engine.
// `may` measured by direct grep of `src/frob/fleet/__init__.py`: `fs`
// (`load_manifest`'s `path.open("rb")` + `tomllib.load` of the tracked
// `fleet.toml` -- declared as the plain `fs` kind, not `fs-read`: this
// scanner's `fs-read` needle only fires on `Path.read_text`-shaped reads,
// which `open("rb")`/`tomllib.load` is not, so declaring `fs-read` here
// trips SYS101 as a capability this scanner never actually reports for
// this file, same precision lesson `registry_model` above already
// documents for `fs-write`), `exec` (`_git_branch_and_dirty`/
// `_gate_summary_probe` both `subprocess.run` a FIXED argv --
// `["git", "status", ...]` and `["uv", "run", "--project", <path>, "frob",
// "check", "--json"]` -- built from this repo's own manifest-declared
// sibling paths, never from response/network bytes). No `eval`/`net`/
// `sql`/`fetch_url`/`ffi`/`deserialize` call exists anywhere under this
// package (verified by direct grep) -- `json.loads` of a LOCAL
// subprocess's own stdout is not a `deserialize`-kind sink under this
// model's vocabulary (no untrusted-network byte stream reaches it).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node fleet : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/fleet/**";
    may "exec";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
    may "fs";
    // T-0778: T-0200's kill switch (frob.process._guard.
    // guarded_subprocess_run) is real and shipped, but this node's own
    // `subprocess.run` call sites (src/frob/fleet/__init__.py, not the
    // git seam T-0778 wired) are not yet routed through it -- an honest
    // remaining gap, not a pending mechanism. Follow-on: T-0803.
    // T-0724: same SYS203/tickets_ledger disposition as `cli` above --
    // the `.frob/tickets.lock` flock (T-0458/T-0633) is the real,
    // untracked-by-the-grammar arbiter; re-evaluate at T-0700.
    waive "SYS203:tickets_ledger" reason "all ledger writers serialize through .frob/tickets.lock (T-0458/T-0633); mode-blind until T-0700 arbitrated_by can express this -- re-evaluate at T-0700" ticket "T-0700";
}

// dup+frob-core: everything that is real infrastructure but not one of
// the named components above -- src/frob/dup/**, logging/**, process/**,
// gitlog/**, gitio.py, xref/**, outline/**, map/**, exports/**,
// perf/**, policy/**, release/**, scaffold/**,
// stats/**, bind/**, docs/**, fuzz/**, cycle/**, testing/**, cve/** (T-0150
// self-conformance audit found src/frob/cve/** unmapped -- added here
// rather than as a ninth component for the same density reason).
// T-0440: mutate/**, serve/**, and deploy/** are OFF this node now -- see
// the standalone `mutate`/`serve`/`deploy` nodes below. They used to be
// folded in here for the same "dense utility mesh" reason the rest of
// this list still is, but that framing hid three components with real,
// distinct threat surfaces of their own (mutate spawns the test suite
// under a mutated source file; serve is the one component with an
// external-agent-facing MCP transport boundary; deploy generates and
// remote-execs install/status/uninstall automation on a target host) --
// splitting them out is a modeling-accuracy fix, not a new capability.
// src/frob/tickets/** is NOT here (T-0166 un-fold): T-0150 folded it into
// `core` as a workaround for `store_prop` not accepting `code`/`may` --
// that grammar gap is now fixed (`strata-core/src/parse.rs::parse_store`,
// docs/strata/surface.md#node-grammar-implemented), so `src/frob/
// tickets/**` moved onto the `tickets_ledger` store below, where it
// honestly belongs (the CODE that owns the ticket ledger, not `core`'s).
// Rolled into one node deliberately: these packages form a dense, largely
// acyclic utility mesh (logging alone has ~90 inbound imports repo-wide)
// that does not carve into independently-owned components the way
// gates/check/strata/vet do; modeling each one separately would not
// change any claim in this file, only inflate the flow count.
// T-0155 LINT004: `may "exec"` is a risky capability with no real kill
// switch today -- an honest gap, same posture as `checker` above; waived
// (T-0174) pending T-0200's real kill-switch mechanism, same reason.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
// T-0457/T-0448 (post-merge self-conformance): src/frob/clean/** (tiered
// artifact-only workspace cleanup) and src/frob/render/** (the unified CLI
// output layer) landed without a code= binding anywhere -- SYS102 caught
// both as unmodeled. Folded onto `core` for the same density reason as the
// rest of this node's list rather than as two new components: `clean`
// measures as `fs`/`fs-read` only (`_core.py`'s `shutil.rmtree`/
// `Path.unlink`, `git ls-files` read, `frob.toml` read -- all already
// declared below); `render` measures as `env` only (`_color.py`'s
// `os.environ`/`NO_COLOR`/`CLICOLOR_FORCE` reads, also already declared).
// No new `may` needed for either.
node core : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/dup/**" "src/frob/logging/**" "src/frob/process/**"
        "src/frob/gitlog/**" "src/frob/gitio.py" "src/frob/xref/**"
        "src/frob/outline/**" "src/frob/map/**" "src/frob/exports/**"
        "src/frob/perf/**" "src/frob/policy/**"
        "src/frob/release/**" "src/frob/scaffold/**"
        "src/frob/stats/**" "src/frob/bind/**" "src/frob/docs/**"
        "src/frob/fuzz/**" "src/frob/cycle/**" "src/frob/testing/**"
        "src/frob/cve/**" "src/frob/clean/**"
        "src/frob/render/**";
    may "env";
    may "eval";
    may "exec";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    // T-0778: `src/frob/gitio.py`'s own subprocess spawns are now real,
    // wired kill-switch coverage (frob.process._guard.
    // guarded_subprocess_run, transitively covering the serve daemon and
    // gitio-based lease reads too, T-0440: `serve` now calls this code
    // via a flow edge rather than owning it) -- but this node folds in
    // several OTHER packages (gitlog/**, scaffold/**, testing/**) that
    // still call subprocess.run directly, unguarded. Honest remaining
    // gap, not a pending mechanism. Follow-on: T-0803. (T-0440:
    // `mutate`/`deploy` used to be part of this same honest gap while
    // folded into `core` -- both moved onto their OWN nodes below, and
    // both DO route their subprocess spawns through the real,
    // guarded_subprocess_run kill switch already, unlike the packages
    // still listed here.)
    // T-0724: same SYS203/tickets_ledger disposition as `cli` above --
    // the `.frob/tickets.lock` flock (T-0458/T-0633) is the real,
    // untracked-by-the-grammar arbiter; re-evaluate at T-0700.
    waive "SYS203:tickets_ledger" reason "all ledger writers serialize through .frob/tickets.lock (T-0458/T-0633); mode-blind until T-0700 arbitrated_by can express this -- re-evaluate at T-0700" ticket "T-0700";
}

// T-0440: `frob mutate` -- mutation testing, the honest test-quality
// oracle (src/frob/mutate/__init__.py, T-0011). Split off `core`'s
// former utility-hub node because its real threat surface is distinct:
// it is the one non-`checker`/`vet`/`fleet` component that spawns a
// subprocess whose ARGV embeds a caller-supplied test command
// (`run_mutations`'s `test_argv` parameter), against a source tree it
// has just mutated on disk -- a narrower, more specific CWE-78 exposure
// than the generic "dense utility mesh" framing gave it credit for.
// `may` measured by `frob.strata._effects._line_effects` /
// `frob.vet._capability._scan_directory_capabilities` against
// `src/frob/mutate/**` (T-0440 Done report): `fs` (`_run_mutants`'s
// `target.write_text` of each mutant, restored via the same call in the
// `finally` block) + `fs-read` (`run_mutations`'s `target.read_text` of
// the original source) + `exec` (`_run_mutants`'s
// `guarded_subprocess_run` call, imported from `frob.process._guard`
// inside `_run_mutants` itself -- deferred, but still a real, observed
// `subprocess.` site in this file's own text, hence declared here rather
// than left as an undeclared cross-node effect). No `eval`/`net`/`ffi`/
// `sql`/`fetch_url`/`deserialize`/`env` observed anywhere in this
// package. The exec capability has a REAL, wired kill switch already:
// `run_mutations`'s own module docstring (T-0803) documents that a
// `guarded_subprocess_run` refusal (`FROB_DISABLE_EXEC=1`) aborts the
// WHOLE run with `Err(MutateError.ExecDisabled)` rather than silently
// mis-scoring the refused mutant as "killed" -- the same
// `frob_check_exec_kill_switch` identifier every other
// `guarded_subprocess_run` caller in this model already names, since
// it is the SAME env var / mechanism (`frob.process._guard.
// EXEC_KILL_SWITCH_ENV`), not a second one.
// T-0860: `may "env"` ADDED -- `_run_mutants`'s `child_env = {**os.environ,
// MUTATION_RUN_ENV: "1"}` reads the whole parent environment to build the
// mutant subprocess's own env, a genuine (not false-positive) `os.environ`
// site that postdates the T-0440 survey above and was never folded back
// into this node's declared capability set, tripping SYS100 (`env`
// observed but undeclared). No further "No `eval`/`net`/`ffi`/`sql`/
// `fetch_url`/`deserialize` observed" claim above is affected -- this is
// the one addition.
// frob:doc docs/modules/mutate.md#public-api
// frob:ticket T-0860
node mutate : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/mutate/**";
    may "exec";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    may "env";
}

// T-0864: `frob natives build` -- frob-owned `maturin develop` per
// declared `[[native]]` rust crate, sharing one git-common-dir-keyed
// `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` (T-0732's verified design, moved out of this repo's
// own Makefile). `may` measured directly against `src/frob/natives/**`:
// `exec` (`build_natives`'s own `guarded_subprocess_run` call spawning
// `uvx maturin develop`, the same real, wired `frob_check_exec_kill_switch`
// mechanism every other `guarded_subprocess_run` caller in this model
// already names) + `env` (`os.environ.copy()` to build the maturin
// subprocess's own env with `VIRTUAL_ENV`/`CARGO_TARGET_DIR` overlaid). No
// `fs`/`fs-read`: `_crate_dir_for`'s `Path.is_file()` is a plain
// existence stat, never an actual file open/read -- the real
// `frob.toml`/crate-tree reads are `frob.testing._runners.load_natives`'s
// own `core`-node capability, reached via the `f_natives_core` flow
// below, not this node's own effect. No `eval`/`net`/`ffi`/`sql`/
// `fetch_url`/`deserialize` observed either. Split onto its own node
// rather than folded into `cli` (which already owns
// `src/frob/app/natives_runner.py` via its `src/frob/app/**` glob) --
// same "code owns the effect, not its caller" shape as
// `mutate`/`deploy`/`serve` above: the CLI wiring is a thin dispatcher,
// this package is where the real capability lives.
// frob:doc docs/modules/cli.md#frob-natives-build-t-0864
// frob:ticket T-0864
node natives : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/natives/**";
    may "exec";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
    may "env";
}

// T-0440: `frob serve` -- the MCP adapter exposing frob's enforcement
// queries over stdio (src/frob/serve/**, docs/modules/serve.md). Split
// off `core`'s former utility-hub node because it is the ONE component
// in this whole model an external MCP CLIENT PROCESS talks to directly
// (`server.py`'s stdio `FastMCP` transport, `run_stdio`) -- a real,
// previously-undeclared boundary the coarse hub framing hid entirely, no
// different in kind from `registry` being the network boundary `vet`
// crosses. The transport itself is stdio only (no listening socket, no
// `net` capability observed or plausible: `server.py`/`_tools.py`/
// `_warm.py`/`_daemon.py`'s own bound code, per `frob.vet._capability.
// _scan_directory_capabilities` and `frob.strata._effects._line_effects`
// against `src/frob/serve/**` (T-0440 Done report), observe ZERO net/fs/
// exec/eval effects of their own -- every filesystem read
// (`.frob/cache.db`), git subprocess call (`_daemon.py`'s
// `poll_rebase_bot`), and gate/graph/ticket read this component performs
// is delegated to `frob.graph`/`frob.gates`/`frob.gitio`/`frob.testing`/
// `frob.tickets` code that lives on OTHER nodes -- modeled below as real
// flow edges into `core`/`gates`/`graphlang`/`tickets_ledger`, not as
// `serve`'s own capabilities (the same "code owns the effect, not its
// caller" discipline `core`'s `f_core_tickets` comment already
// documents). This is a genuinely zero-`may` node: no declared
// capability at all, same shape as the `registry` boundary node above
// (minus the `foreign` clearance -- `serve` is trusted, internal code,
// just with no risky effect of its own to declare).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node serve : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/serve/**";

    // T-0756: same SYS203/tickets_ledger disposition as `cli`/`gates`/
    // `fleet`/`core` above (T-0724) -- `serve` also has a Flow into
    // `tickets_ledger` (`f_serve_tickets` below) via `frob.tickets`'s own
    // locked writer path, so SYS203's mode-blind "any inbound flow counted
    // as a write" join fires here too. This waiver was missing purely
    // because `serve` was added after T-0724's original sweep across its
    // four sibling nodes, not because the underlying contention model
    // differs -- discovered when T-0756 folded SYS203 into `frob check`'s
    // own gate pipeline (SELFAUDIT001) for the first time and this exact
    // gap surfaced as a real, previously-undisclosed red `frob sys audit`.
    waive "SYS203:tickets_ledger" reason "all ledger writers serialize through .frob/tickets.lock (T-0458/T-0633); mode-blind until T-0700 arbitrated_by can express this -- re-evaluate at T-0700" ticket "T-0700";
}

// T-0440: `frob deploy` -- compiles `std.host` manifests into idempotent
// install/status/uninstall automation (bash + PowerShell/DSC) and runs
// the `frob deploy audit --vm` snapshot-diff harness against a live
// target host over ssh/VBoxManage (src/frob/deploy/**, deploy epic
// T-0254). Split off `core`'s former utility-hub node because its real
// threat surface is distinct from the rest of that mesh: it is the one
// component whose generated OUTPUT is itself install/uninstall
// automation meant to run with host-admin privilege on a REMOTE target,
// and whose own `_vm_runner.py` spawns `ssh`/`VBoxManage` subprocesses
// against that target directly.
// `may` measured by `frob.strata._effects._line_effects` /
// `frob.vet._capability._scan_directory_capabilities` against
// `src/frob/deploy/**` (T-0440 Done report): `fs` (`_drift.py`'s
// `toml_path.open("rb")` -- the scanner's `open(`-shaped needle, same
// precision note `registry_model`/`fleet` above already document: this
// declares as the plain `fs` kind, not `fs-write`, matching every other
// node/store in this model) + `fs-read` (`_drift.py`'s
// `committed_path.read_text`) + `exec` (`_vm_runner.py`'s
// `_run_guarded`, routed through `guarded_subprocess_run` -- the SAME
// real, wired `frob.process._guard.EXEC_KILL_SWITCH_ENV` kill switch
// every other `guarded_subprocess_run` caller in this model names via
// `frob_check_exec_kill_switch`). `may "eval"` is deliberately NOT
// declared: the scanner's bare-`eval(`-shaped needle matches
// `_conform.py::_mutation_for_eval`'s own PYTHON FUNCTION NAME (the
// substring "eval(" inside the identifier `_mutation_for_eval(`), not a
// call to the `eval`/`exec` builtins -- the exact T-0151-class
// self-match false positive `gates`'s own header comment above already
// documents for a different needle (`compile(`); direct grep of
// `src/frob/deploy/**` confirms zero real `eval(`/`exec(` builtin calls
// anywhere in this package, so declaring `may "eval"` here would be an
// unfalsifiable claim SYS101 exists to catch, not an honest one. No
// `net`/`ffi`/`sql`/`fetch_url`/`deserialize`/`env` observed either --
// the ssh/VBoxManage calls are modeled as `exec` (a local subprocess
// spawn), the same convention `fleet`'s ssh-adjacent git/uv subprocess
// calls above already use, not as a separate `net` capability.
// T-0882: the T-0860 waiver below is REMOVED -- `frob.vet._capability`'s
// scanner (`_needle_hits_as_bare_call`/`_BARE_CALL_NEEDLES`) no longer
// treats `eval(`/`exec(` as a plain substring, so it no longer self-matches
// `_conform.py::_mutation_for_eval`'s own function name (the identifier
// merely ENDS in the text "eval(", it is not a bare builtin call site).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
// frob:ticket T-0860
node deploy : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/deploy/**";
    may "exec";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
}

// vet: src/frob/vet/**. Dependency-vetting -- the ONE component that
// crosses the trust boundary out to `registry` (network fetches for
// publish dates, OSV advisories, popularity data).
// T-0158: `may "fetch_url"` added -- urlopen-shaped URL fetches
// (_nvd.py/_registry.py), a real observed capability the old coarser
// pattern table never patterned, not new code.
// T-0201: `may "deserialize"`/`may "eval"`/`may "exec"`/`may "sql"`
// REMOVED. A T-0150-round-2 prototype (reverted at the time) first showed
// these four only ever fired via `_capability.py`/`_capability_registry.py`/
// `_cve_fingerprint.py` scanning THEIR OWN needle-literal tables -- the
// T-0151 self-match class, not real vet code exercising the capability
// (`_pickle_violation` in `_ecosystem.py` DETECTS shipped pickle files by
// extension, it never itself calls `pickle.load`; `conn.execute(...)` in
// `_cache.py`/`_nvd.py`/`_registry.py` is parameterized, not the
// string-formatted `execute(f"...")` shape the `sql` needle patterns for;
// no `eval`/`exec` call exists anywhere under `src/frob/vet/**` outside
// the excluded catalog files). Once T-0201's shared `is_self_pattern_path`
// exclusion (`frob.vet._capability`) is applied consistently in BOTH vet
// aggregation and every `frob.strata._selfconform`/`_effects` join path,
// a clean self-conformance scan confirms zero real sites for these four --
// they were never genuinely exercised capabilities, so removing them is
// the honest fix, not a coverage regression.
// T-0822 (closing T-0155's honest LINT004 gap, same disposition
// as `checker` above; supersedes T-0803's still-unwired disposition and
// the never-filed "T-0817" waiver id a stale dispatch prompt referenced):
// `may "net"` now has a REAL, checked-in kill switch --
// `frob.process.net_enabled()` (`FROB_DISABLE_NET`, T-0200's mechanism,
// unused pending a real call site until this ticket wired it). Both of
// vet's own `urlopen` sites (`_registry.py::_result_from_network`,
// `_nvd.py::_fetch_from_network`) check `net_enabled()` before ever
// opening a socket and degrade exactly like a network failure
// (`ok=False`) when disabled -- live and without a redeploy
// (`tests/test_vet.py::TestRegistryLookup`), not an aspirational claim
// (T-0150/T-0151 discipline), so the T-0174 `waive "LINT004"` is retired
// in favor of the real declaration below.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
node vet : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    code "src/frob/vet/**";
    may "env";
    may "fetch_url";
    may "ffi";
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    may "install-hook";
    may "net";
    attr flag=frob_vet_net_kill_switch;
}

// The ticket ledger: tickets.md + tickets/, git-tracked truth.
// `code "src/frob/tickets/**"` (T-0166 un-fold): `store_prop` in
// `strata-core/src/parse.rs::parse_store` used to reject `code`/`may` (the
// real, narrow gap T-0150 found and worked around by folding this code
// into `core` instead); T-0166 fixed the grammar (mirrors `node`'s
// `code`/`may`, docs/strata/surface.md#node-grammar-implemented), so the
// ledger's own code now binds here, where it honestly belongs, rather
// than on a neighboring node. `may` was measured the same honest way the
// module docstring's header describes for every other node/store here
// (`frob.vet._capability.scan_directory_capabilities` against
// `src/frob/tickets/**`: `env` (`clipboard.py` env probing),
// `exec` (`clipboard.py`'s `subprocess.run` calls to `wl-paste`/platform
// clipboard tools), `fs` (`_store.py`/`_land.py` ledger file writes) --
// zero `eval`/`net`/`ffi` observed). `core`'s existing
// `f_core_tickets: core -> tickets_ledger` flow (and the cli/gates
// equivalents) still model the real cross-component calls INTO this
// code, unchanged by where the code itself is bound; `append_only`
// because the ledger is edited by appending new tickets and updating
// ticket bodies in place under git history, never by deleting the record
// of what happened (archive moves closed tickets to a separate tracked
// file, `write_archive`, rather than erasing them).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
store tickets_ledger : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    engine git_tracked;
    append_only;
    code "src/frob/tickets/**";
    may "env";
    // `may "eval"`: `_store.py`'s posix-only `fcntl` import goes through
    // `importlib.import_module("fcntl")` (T-0458 ledger_lock) rather than a
    // plain `import fcntl`, so `try`/`except ImportError` can degrade to a
    // documented no-op on a platform lacking the module -- vet's `eval`
    // pattern table fires on `importlib.import_module(` regardless of
    // whether the argument is a runtime string or (as here) a fixed
    // literal; the module name is never attacker-influenceable.
    may "eval";
    may "exec";
    attr flag=frob_check_exec_kill_switch;
    may "fs";
    may "fs-read";
    // T-0166 LINT004: `may "exec"` is a risky capability. T-0200 built a
    // real kill switch and T-0778 wired the git seam (`frob.gitio.
    // run_argv`, which the serve daemon and gitio-based lease reads reuse
    // transitively) through it -- but this node's own `exec` sites (`git
    // ls-files` in `tickets/__init__.py::_repo_files_git`, the
    // shell=True evidence-command spawn, `clipboard.py`'s subprocess.run
    // calls) were wired through the guard by T-0803/T-0805, so the
    // declared flag below is a true statement of switchable capability.
}

// The `.frob/` symbol-graph cache (`frob.graph`'s snapshot cache): derived
// entirely from a `graphlang` parse and invalidated by the next one, never
// hand-edited -- staleness is bounded by "no parse has happened since the
// last one", modeled here as a 1s ttl (parse-triggered invalidation is
// effectively immediate, not time-based, but the cache sugar requires a
// bound and 1s documents "as fresh as the last parse" without claiming a
// false 0).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
cache graph_cache of graphlang {
    ttl 1 s;
    invalidate_on f_parse;
}

// -- flows ---------------------------------------------------------------

// vet's one network-facing edge (src/frob/vet/_registry.py::
// _result_from_network, _osv.py, _popular_*.py). T-0155 LINT001: a
// public/edge boundary accepting external (foreign-trust) flows needs a
// declared rate limit; `_registry.py`'s own client throttles registry
// lookups well under 1 req/s in steady state (per-dependency lookups,
// not a hot path), so 1 req/s is an honest, conservative declared bound,
// not an aspirational one.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_registry_fetch : registry -> vet { label Public; rate 1 req/s; attr timeout; }

// cli dispatches into every other component (src/frob/app/app.py::
// _dispatch_table + the runner modules under src/frob/app/).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_parse : cli -> graphlang { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_core : cli -> core { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_checker : cli -> checker { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_gates : cli -> gates { label Internal; attr local; }
// The ledger write path a ticket-mutating subcommand takes
// (src/frob/app/ticket_runner.py -> src/frob/tickets/_store.py::
// write_ticket). See the `frob:channel f_cli_tickets` anchor there.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_tickets : cli -> tickets_ledger { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_vet : cli -> vet { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0707: `src/frob/app/registry_runner.py` imports `frob.registry`
// directly (the `frob registry` subcommand's CLI entrypoint).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_registry_model : cli -> registry_model { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0707: `src/frob/app/fleet_runner.py` imports `frob.fleet` directly
// (the `frob fleet` subcommand's CLI entrypoint).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_fleet : cli -> fleet { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0440: `src/frob/app/deploy_runner.py` (the `frob deploy` subcommand)
// and `src/frob/app/check_runner.py` (DEPLOY001/DEPLOY002's
// `deploy_drift_violations`/`deploy_conformance_violations`) both import
// `frob.deploy` directly.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_deploy : cli -> deploy { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0440: `src/frob/app/mutate_runner.py` imports `frob.mutate` directly
// (the `frob mutate` subcommand's CLI entrypoint).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_mutate : cli -> mutate { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0864: `src/frob/app/natives_runner.py` imports `frob.natives` directly
// (the `frob natives build` subcommand's CLI entrypoint).
// frob:doc docs/modules/cli.md#frob-natives-build-t-0864
flow f_cli_natives : cli -> natives { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0440: `src/frob/app/serve_runner.py` imports `frob.serve` directly
// (the `frob serve` subcommand's CLI entrypoint).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_cli_serve : cli -> serve { label Internal; attr local; }

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_graphlang_core : graphlang -> core { label Internal; attr local; }

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_checker_graphlang : checker -> graphlang { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_checker_core : checker -> core { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_checker_gates : checker -> gates { label Internal; attr local; }

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_core_graphlang : core -> graphlang { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_core_gates : core -> gates { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_core_tickets : core -> tickets_ledger { label Internal; attr local; }

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_gates_graphlang : gates -> graphlang { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_gates_core : gates -> core { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_gates_strata : gates -> stratamod { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_gates_tickets : gates -> tickets_ledger { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0707: `src/frob/gates/_registry_exhaustiveness.py::registry_gate`
// imports `frob.registry._models`/`frob.registry._staleness` directly --
// the one consumer of the unified registry model.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_gates_registry_model : gates -> registry_model { label Internal; attr local; }

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_strata_graphlang : stratamod -> graphlang { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_strata_core : stratamod -> core { label Internal; attr local; }
// strata's `_effects.py` reuses `frob.vet._capability`'s pattern tables
// (docstring, T-0079) -- the one strata -> vet edge.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_strata_vet : stratamod -> vet { label Internal; attr local; }

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_vet_gates : vet -> gates { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_vet_graphlang : vet -> graphlang { label Internal; attr local; }
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_vet_core : vet -> core { label Internal; attr local; }

// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_tickets_core : tickets_ledger -> core { label Internal; attr local; }

// T-0440: `frob.deploy._generate`/`_generate_windows.py` import
// `frob.strata`/`frob.strata._host`/`frob.strata._krb` directly (reads
// `HostManifest`/`HostAcl`/`krb_manifest_for` to compile a design model
// into install automation).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_deploy_strata : deploy -> stratamod { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0440: `frob.deploy._vm_runner` imports `frob.process._guard`
// (`EXEC_KILL_SWITCH_ENV`/`guarded_subprocess_run`) directly.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_deploy_core : deploy -> core { label Internal; attr local; }

// T-0440: `frob.mutate._run_mutants` imports `frob.process._guard.
// guarded_subprocess_run` (deferred, inside the function -- still a
// real call).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_mutate_core : mutate -> core { label Internal; attr local; }

// T-0864: `frob.natives._build.build_natives` imports `frob.gitio.
// git_common_dir` (T-0732's shared-cache keying) and `frob.testing.
// _runners.load_natives` (T-0333's `[[native]]` parser), both `core`'s
// own code.
// frob:doc docs/modules/cli.md#frob-natives-build-t-0864
flow f_natives_core : natives -> core { label Internal; attr local; }

// T-0440: `frob.serve._daemon`/`_warm.py` import `frob.gitio.run_argv`
// (git subprocess reads: `poll_rebase_bot`'s `git merge-tree` simulation)
// and `frob.testing.collect_python_tests` directly.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_serve_core : serve -> core { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0440: `frob.serve._warm` imports `frob.gates.load_baseline` directly
// (the stamped-baseline half of a warm `WarmState`).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_serve_gates : serve -> gates { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0440: `frob.serve._warm`/`_tools.py` import `frob.graph`'s
// `build_graph`/`affects`/`edges_from`/`edges_to`/`load_graph`/`resolve`
// and `frob.graph.lock`'s `drift`/`load_lock` directly.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_serve_graphlang : serve -> graphlang { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0440: `frob.serve._tools`/`_daemon.py` import `frob.tickets`'s
// `doable`/`load_queue`/`read_all_leases` directly (the doable-tickets
// MCP query and the rebase-bot's in-flight-lease scan).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_serve_tickets : serve -> tickets_ledger { label Internal; attr local; }

// T-0707: `frob.fleet.route_ticket` imports `frob.tickets` directly
// (`TicketSpec`/`doable`/`load_queue`/`new_ticket`) to file a finding into
// a sibling repo's own ledger.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_fleet_tickets : fleet -> tickets_ledger { label Internal; attr local; }
// T-0707: `frob.fleet` imports `frob.logging.get_logger` (module logger
// setup, owned by `core`'s `src/frob/logging/**` glob).
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
flow f_fleet_core : fleet -> core { label Internal; attr local; }

// -- boundary --------------------------------------------------------

// `_result_from_network` parses and validates the registry response
// before it becomes a `RegistryResult` anything downstream trusts; that
// validation IS the endorsement (see the `frob:boundary b_vet_endorse`
// anchor at src/frob/vet/_registry.py::_result_from_network). Nothing
// downstream of `vet` re-validates a registry response, so if this
// boundary is ever deleted the taint closure below (`c_no_registry_
// ledger`) must -- and will -- flip to REFUTED.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
boundary b_vet_endorse endorse f_registry_fetch : foreign -> trusted when "checksum_verified"

// -- claims --------------------------------------------------------

// Supply-chain integrity: raw third-party registry content must never
// reach the tracked ticket ledger unendorsed. Proves only because
// `b_vet_endorse` stops the taint closure at the registry -> vet hop;
// delete that boundary (or the `frob:boundary` anchor that attests it in
// code) and the witness path registry -> vet -> gates -> tickets_ledger
// refutes it, which is the whole point of drawing this claim.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
assert c_no_registry_ledger noflow registry -> tickets_ledger

// The symbol-graph cache is derivable, not a second source of truth: its
// only freshness bound is "as old as the last parse", asserted here as
// <= 5s (the 1s ttl declared above with headroom, not a tight SLA -- the
// claim is "bounded and cheap to recompute", not "sub-second").
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
assert c_cache_derivable bound age graph_cache <= 5 s

// Sanity check that the gate suite's findings CAN reach the ledger
// through the layer that actually writes to it (cli's ticket_runner),
// i.e. this model has not accidentally drawn gates as a dead end.
// frob:doc docs/strata/roadmap.md#self-hosting-commitments-decision-d7
assert c_gates_reach_tickets reach gates -> tickets_ledger

// T-0150: declaring `may "exec"` on checker/core/vet (measured honestly
// from real `scan_file_capabilities` sites) drags in a THREAT003 CWE-78
// (OS-command-injection) discharge obligation per node
// (docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations) -- these three
// `assume` claims discharge it, each with the SPECIFIC reason a human
// (not the closure engine) is attesting no exploitable OS-command
// injection exists, honestly stated rather than shaped to pass:
//
// checker: `assert` was tried first and PROVES clean -- no flow reaches
// checker from `registry` at all (`f_vet_gates`/`f_vet_graphlang`/
// `f_vet_core` exist, no `vet -> checker` edge), so this is provably true,
// not merely assumed... except an `assume` (not `assert`) is used anyway
// for uniformity with core/vet below, since all three obligations share
// one discharge shape and one review cadence; the graph-provable case is
// exactly as safe assumed as asserted.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:checker" noflow registry -> checker owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// core: registry DOES reach core transitively (`f_registry_fetch: registry
// -> vet`, `f_vet_core: vet -> core`), so a plain `assert noflow` here
// would be REFUTED -- an `assume` is the honest tool, not a workaround:
// every exec call site under `core`'s `code=` globs
// (src/frob/gitio.py, src/frob/perf/**,
// src/frob/gitlog/**) invokes a FIXED, hardcoded argv list (git/cargo
// subcommands) built from this repo's own config, never from registry
// response bytes -- the registry->vet->core path carries dependency
// METADATA (name/version/hashes, already checksum-endorsed by
// `b_vet_endorse`), not command arguments, so it cannot reach any of
// these argv lists. Reviewed 2026-07. (`src/frob/tickets/clipboard.py`'s
// exec call site moved to `tickets_ledger`'s own discharge below, T-0166
// un-fold. T-0440: `src/frob/mutate/**`'s exec call site moved to
// `mutate`'s own discharge below, same un-fold reasoning.)
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:core" noflow registry -> core owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// tickets_ledger: T-0166 un-fold moved `src/frob/tickets/clipboard.py`'s
// `subprocess.run` exec call site (`wl-paste`/platform clipboard probing,
// a FIXED hardcoded argv, never registry-derived) onto this store's own
// `code`/`may`, dragging in its own CWE-78 obligation. `c_no_registry_
// ledger` above already ASSERTS (and the closure proves) that registry
// cannot reach `tickets_ledger` at all -- a graph-provable NoFlow, same
// as `checker`'s case above -- but THREAT003's discharge check requires
// an `assume` here too: an `assert`ed claim still has to pass the
// boundary-KIND (mitigation-chokepoint) proof (no ENDORSE boundary sits
// on this non-existent path to be of any kind), an exemption only
// `assume`/`managed` get (docs/strata/threat.md#phasing item C), so this
// follows the SAME `assume`-for-uniformity precedent `checker`'s comment
// above documents -- the graph-provable case is exactly as safe assumed
// as asserted.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:tickets_ledger" noflow registry -> tickets_ledger owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// vet: registry flows directly into vet (`f_registry_fetch`). The ONLY
// file under vet's `code=` glob matching the scanner's `exec` needle is
// `src/frob/vet/_capability.py` itself, and its ONLY match is the literal
// STRING `"subprocess."` stored as pattern-table DATA
// (`_PATTERNS["python"]["exec"]`) -- there is no `subprocess.run`/`os.
// system` CALL anywhere in `src/frob/vet/*.py` (verified by direct grep,
// T-0150's Done report). This is the scanner-self-match artifact filed as
// a separate ticket in this ticket's Done report (vet's own pattern
// tables trivially match themselves) -- not fixed here, per that ticket's
// scope, but honestly discharged here rather than left dangling.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:vet" noflow registry -> vet owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// T-0707: `fleet` newly declares `may "exec"` (fixed-argv `subprocess.run`
// for local `git status`/`frob check --json` probes), dragging in the same
// CWE-78 discharge obligation as core/vet/tickets_ledger above. No flow
// exists from `registry` (the foreign package-registry node) to `fleet` at
// all -- `fleet` only talks to `cli`, `tickets_ledger`, and `core` -- so
// this is the same graph-provable NoFlow case those three nodes' comments
// already document; `assume` used for the same uniformity reason (the
// THREAT003 discharge check requires a boundary-kind proof only `assume`/
// `managed` are exempt from). Every `subprocess.run` argv under `fleet`'s
// code glob is a hardcoded list (`["git", "status", ...]`,
// `["uv", "run", "--project", <manifest-declared sibling path>, "frob",
// "check", "--json"]`) built from `fleet.toml`'s own tracked repo paths,
// never from registry response bytes. Reviewed 2026-07.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:fleet" noflow registry -> fleet owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// T-0440: `mutate` newly declares `may "exec"` (the un-folded T-0011
// mutation-test-runner subprocess spawn, `_run_mutants`'s
// `guarded_subprocess_run` call), dragging in the same CWE-78 discharge
// obligation as core/vet/tickets_ledger/fleet above. No flow exists from
// `registry` to `mutate` at all -- `mutate` only talks to `cli` (inbound)
// and `core` (outbound) -- so this is the same graph-provable NoFlow case
// those nodes' comments already document; `assume` used for the same
// uniformity reason. The `test_argv` this node's ONE exec call site
// spawns is caller-supplied (not registry-derived: it comes from
// `frob.testing`'s touched-set test collection, driven by this repo's
// OWN tracked test tree), and the source file it mutates is read from
// this repo's OWN tracked tree, never from a registry response. Reviewed
// 2026-07-23.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:mutate" noflow registry -> mutate owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// T-0864: `natives` newly declares `may "exec"` (`build_natives`'s
// `guarded_subprocess_run` call spawning `uvx maturin develop`), dragging
// in the same CWE-78 discharge obligation as core/vet/tickets_ledger/
// fleet/mutate above. No flow exists from `registry` to `natives` at all
// -- `natives` only talks to `cli` (inbound) and `core` (outbound) -- so
// this is the same graph-provable NoFlow case those nodes' comments
// already document; `assume` used for the same uniformity reason. The
// ONE exec call site's argv is built entirely from `frob.toml`'s own
// tracked `[[native]]` entries (`spec.name`, resolved to a crate
// directory via a fixed underscore/hyphen convention checked against
// this repo's OWN tracked filesystem) plus a hardcoded `maturin develop`
// flag list -- never from registry response bytes. Reviewed 2026-07-26.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:natives" noflow registry -> natives owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// T-0440: `deploy` newly declares `may "exec"` (`_vm_runner.py`'s
// ssh/VBoxManage `guarded_subprocess_run` calls), dragging in the same
// CWE-78 discharge obligation as core/vet/tickets_ledger/fleet/mutate
// above. No flow exists from `registry` to `deploy` at all -- `deploy`
// only talks to `cli` (inbound) and `core`/`stratamod` (outbound) -- so
// this is the same graph-provable NoFlow case those nodes' comments
// already document; `assume` used for the same uniformity reason. Every
// `_vm_runner.py` argv (`ssh <host> ...`, `VBoxManage ...`) is built from
// this repo's own tracked `std.host` manifest fields (hostname/vm name),
// not from registry response bytes -- `deploy`'s only flows are outbound
// (`f_deploy_core`, `f_deploy_strata`), so no inbound path from
// `registry` reaches this node's argv construction at all. Reviewed
// 2026-07-23.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:deploy" noflow registry -> deploy owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// T-0401 (docs/audits/strata.md G3): `eval` used to be globally
// `BenignCapability`-excused ("no CWE_CATALOG entry targets dynamic code
// evaluation") -- false, CWE-94 IS that entry, it just was not joined to
// the `eval` capability kind. Removing the excuse means every `may "eval"`
// declaration above (cli/graphlang/stratamod/core/tickets_ledger) now
// drags in a real THREAT003 CWE-94 discharge obligation, same shape as
// the CWE-78 block above. Each is discharged honestly below:
//
// cli/graphlang/stratamod/core: T-0150/T-0151 already established that
// this repo's `eval` measurement is a scanner-precision artifact -- vet's
// `eval` pattern needle matches a bare `compile(`/`importlib.import_
// module(` call regardless of whether the argument is attacker-
// influenceable, and every measured site under these four nodes' `code=`
// globs is `re.compile(...)` (regex compilation, not dynamic code
// evaluation) or a fixed-literal `importlib.import_module(...)` (see
// tickets_ledger's own comment above); no genuine `eval(...)`/exec-string
// site exists under any of these globs (verified by direct grep, same
// standard T-0150/T-0151's Done reports already applied to `exec`/`sql`
// above). Filed as its own scanner-precision ticket (T-0151-class,
// unfixed here per that ticket's scope) rather than silently narrowed
// away.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-94:cli" noflow registry -> cli owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-94:graphlang" noflow registry -> graphlang owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-94:stratamod" noflow registry -> stratamod owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-94:core" noflow registry -> core owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// gates: T-0443 gave `gates` its own `may "eval"` (importlib.import_module
// of the `[[docblocks.commands]]` parser factory in gates/_docblocks.py),
// dragging in fresh CWE-94/CWE-78 obligations. The import target is a
// dotted path read from TRACKED frob.toml -- never from registry response
// bytes, user input, or scanned file content -- and no subprocess/
// os.system/shell call site exists under gates' code glob (verified by
// direct grep, same T-0150/T-0151 standard as the nodes above), so no
// code-injection or OS-command path from registry can reach it.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-94:gates" noflow registry -> gates owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:gates" noflow registry -> gates owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// `eval` also joins `CWE-78` WITHIN `CWE_CATALOG` itself (the `owasp-
// top-10` default view does not include CWE-94 at all, G6 -- a separate
// disclosed gap), so cli/graphlang/stratamod each ALSO drag in a fresh
// CWE-78 obligation they did not have before (none of the three declare
// `may "exec"`, only `eval`) -- core/tickets_ledger already carry a
// `weakness:CWE-78:<node>` claim from their `exec` capability above, so
// this one join covers both firings for those two; the same reasoning
// (bare `compile(`/`importlib.import_module(` scanner match, no genuine
// dynamic-code-exec site) applies here as it does to the CWE-94 claims
// just above.
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:cli" noflow registry -> cli owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:graphlang" noflow registry -> graphlang owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-78:stratamod" noflow registry -> stratamod owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// tickets_ledger: same "provably NoFlow via c_no_registry_ledger, assume
// anyway for uniformity with the discharge shape THREAT003 requires"
// precedent the CWE-78 block above already documents for this node;
// `_store.py`'s `importlib.import_module("fcntl")` is a fixed literal
// module name, never attacker-influenceable (see the `may "eval"` comment
// on this store above).
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-94:tickets_ledger" noflow registry -> tickets_ledger owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// T-0158: `may "sql"` newly declared for graphlang/vet (the exhaustive
// dangerous-operations registry now patterns sqlite3 string-formatted
// execute() calls it never did before) drags in CWE-89 (SQLi) and CWE-639
// (quality's reuse of the same sql capability join) discharge obligations
// for both nodes. Each is discharged honestly below, not narrowed away:
//
// graphlang: the ONLY `execute(f"...")` call under graphlang's `code=`
// glob is `src/frob/graph/cache.py::_ensure_schema`'s
// `conn.execute(f"DROP TABLE IF EXISTS {table}")` -- `table` iterates a
// FIXED, hardcoded tuple literal ("meta", "files", "symbols", "edges",
// "malformed") defined two lines above the call site, never external
// input; every OTHER `execute()` call in this file already uses `?`
// parameter binding (verified by direct grep, T-0158 Done report).
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-89:graphlang" noflow registry -> graphlang owner logan review "2026-10-15"
// frob:doc docs/strata/threat.md#capabilities-drag-in-obligations
assume "weakness:CWE-639:graphlang" noflow registry -> graphlang owner logan review "2026-10-15"

// vet: the ONLY file under vet's `code=` glob matching the scanner's sql/
// fetch_url/deserialize needles is `src/frob/vet/_capability_registry.py`
// itself -- its `DANGEROUS_OPERATIONS` table stores every needle
// (`execute(f"`, `urllib.`, `pickle.load(`, ...) as literal string DATA,
// the SAME scanner-self-match artifact T-0150/T-0151 already documented
// for `_capability.py`'s old hand-written pattern tables (T-0158 replaced
// those tables with this registry; the self-match class moved file but
// did not change shape). Every REAL `execute()`/`urlopen()`/`pickle.
// load()` call in `src/frob/vet/*.py` outside this one registry file
// either uses `?` parameter binding (`_registry.py`, `_cache.py`) or does
